<reading-questions xml:id="reflections-elem-matrices">
<title>Reflect on your understanding</title>

<introduction><p>
	In addition to the reflection activities below,
	re-read <xref ref="section-elem-matrices-terminology" text="type-global-title" />.
	Be sure you understand the new definitions introduced in this chapter,
	and spend some time committing it to memory.
</p></introduction>

<exercise xml:id="reflection-elem-matrices-role">
	<title>Elementary matrices</title>
	<p> Explain the role that <term>elementary matrices</term> play in matrix algebra. </p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Inverses of elementary matrices</title>
	<p>
	In the context of your response to <xref ref="reflection-elem-matrices-role" text="type-local" />,
	explain:
	<ol>
		<li> Why every elementary matrix is invertible.                  </li>
		<li>
			Why the inverse of an elementary matrix is also elementary,
			of the same type.
		</li>
		<li> What the inverse of an elementary matrix represents.        </li>
	</ol>
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Linking elementary matrices to the theory of invertible matrices</title>
	<p> Briefly summarize the main idea(s) behind each step in the proof of <xref ref="theorem-elem-matrices-equiv-to-invertible" />. </p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Uniqueness of decompositions</title>
	<p>
	<xref ref="theorem-elem-matrices-equiv-to-invertible-prod-elem">Statement</xref> of <xref ref="theorem-elem-matrices-equiv-to-invertible" />
	says that every invertible matrix can be expressed as a product of elementary matrices.
	For a given invertible matrix,
	is there only <em>one unique</em> such expression,
	or are multiple such expressions possible?
	Explain.
	</p>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="reflection-elem-matrices-revisit-row-equivalence">
	<title>Revisiting row equivalence</title>

	<task xml:id="reflection-elem-matrices-revisit-row-equivalence-reframe"><p>
		Reframe the definition of
		<term><xref ref="definition-row-red-row-equiv" text="custom">row equivalence of matrices</xref></term>
		using the concept of <term>elementary matrices</term>.
	</p></task>

	<task>
		<introduction><p>
			Use your response to <xref ref="reflection-elem-matrices-revisit-row-equivalence-reframe" text="type-local" /> to verify each of the following statements.
		</p></introduction>
		<task><p>
			If matrix <m>B</m> is row equivalent to matrix <m>A</m>,
			then there exists an invertible matrix <m>P</m> so that <m>B = P A</m>.
		</p></task>
		<task><p>
			If there exists an invertible matrix <m>P</m> so that <m>B = P A</m>,
			then matrix <m>B</m> is row equivalent to matrix <m>A</m>.
		</p></task>
	</task>

	<task><p>
		State the relationship between a matrix and its RREF as an algebraic equality involving the matrix, its RREF matrix, and elementary matrices.
	</p></task>

	<task><p> Explain why two square matrices of the same size that are row equivalent must either be both invertible or both singular. </p></task>

</exercise>

</reading-questions>
